<python>
cs_content_header = cs_long_name
</python>

CAT-SOOP stores most persistent activity as **logs** under the configured data
directory.  At the Python level, a log is a sequence of Python objects.  Each
log is identified by three values:

* a database name, usually a username;
* a page path, represented as a list beginning with the course name; and
* a log name, such as `problemstate` or `problemactions`.

Use CAT-SOOP's log tools to read these files.  They are not JSON or text files,
even when a text editor happens to show fragments of readable strings.

<tableofcontents/>

<catsoop-section>Filesystem Layout</catsoop-section>

Suppose:

```python
db_name = "ada"
path = ["numerical_methods", "homework", "hw01"]
logname = "problemactions"
```

With filesystem-backed logging, the corresponding file is:

```text
CS_DATA_ROOT/
└── _logs/
    └── _courses/
        └── numerical_methods/
            └── ada/
                └── homework/
                    └── hw01/
                        └── problemactions.log
```

In general, a course-page log is stored at:

```text
CS_DATA_ROOT/_logs/_courses/COURSE/DB_NAME/PAGE/PATH/LOGNAME.log
```

The first path component becomes the course directory.  The remaining page
components follow the database name.  Logs whose logical path is empty are
instead stored at:

```text
CS_DATA_ROOT/_logs/DB_NAME/LOGNAME.log
```

Several special areas also live below `_logs`.  `_sessions` contains session
records, `_uploads` contains content-addressed uploaded-file data and metadata,
and `_checker` contains work and results for asynchronous grading.  These
special areas do not all use the framed `.log` format described below.

If log encryption is enabled, CAT-SOOP also transforms logical database names,
path components, and log names into opaque names.  In that case, do not try to
construct a physical pathname yourself.  Use `csm_cslog.get_log_filename(...)`
or another `csm_cslog` operation with the logical names.

<catsoop-section>The Binary Log Format</catsoop-section>

Each `.log` file contains one or more framed entries.  An entry consists of:

1. an eight-byte little-endian length;
2. a binary Python pickle, optionally compressed and/or encrypted; and
3. the same eight-byte length again.

CAT-SOOP currently serializes objects with pickle protocol 5.  If
`cs_log_compression` is enabled, it applies LZMA compression.  If the
installation uses `CATSOOP_PASSPHRASE`, it also encrypts the serialized data.
The correct configuration, compression setting, passphrase, and encryption
salt are therefore needed to decode a production log.

<div class="bs-callout bs-callout-warning">
<b>Do not use <code>pickle.load()</code> directly.</b>  It does not understand
CAT-SOOP's framing, compression, or encryption.  Python pickle data can also
execute code while being decoded, so only inspect logs from a CAT-SOOP
installation you trust.
</div>

<catsoop-section>Reading a Log From the Command Line</catsoop-section>

`catsoop logread` decodes every entry and prints a human-readable Python
representation.  A log can be named logically:

```sh
CATSOOP_CONFIG=/etc/catsoop/config.py \
    catsoop logread ada numerical_methods/homework/hw01 problemactions
```

or by its physical filename:

```sh
CATSOOP_CONFIG=/etc/catsoop/config.py \
    catsoop logread \
    /srv/catsoop-data/_logs/_courses/numerical_methods/ada/homework/hw01/problemactions.log
```

Set `CATSOOP_CONFIG` before starting the command so that CAT-SOOP loads the
configuration for the installation containing the log.  If that installation
encrypts logs, also set `CATSOOP_PASSPHRASE` in the command's environment.
Avoid putting a production passphrase directly into shell history; use the
same protected environment mechanism used by the server.

The output is plain, pretty-printed Python data and can be redirected to a text
file for analysis:

```sh
CATSOOP_CONFIG=/etc/catsoop/config.py \
    catsoop logread ada numerical_methods/homework/hw01 problemactions \
    > ada-hw01-actions.txt
```

`catsoop logedit` and `catsoop logwrite` also exist, but both write back to the
live log.  Use them only with a backup and a clear recovery plan.

<catsoop-section>Reading Logs With `csm_cslog`</catsoop-section>

On a course page or in `preload.py`, CAT-SOOP exposes its loaded logging module
as `csm_cslog`.  The main read operations are:

```python
path = ["numerical_methods", "homework", "hw01"]

# All entries, in chronological order.
actions = csm_cslog.read_log("ada", path, "problemactions")

# Only the newest entry, or {} when the log does not exist.
state = csm_cslog.most_recent("ada", path, "problemstate", {})

# The physical file used by the filesystem backend.
filename = csm_cslog.get_log_filename("ada", path, "problemstate")
```

Useful write operations include:

```python
csm_cslog.update_log(db_name, path, logname, new_entry)
csm_cslog.overwrite_log(db_name, path, logname, replacement)
csm_cslog.delete_log(db_name, path, logname)
```

`update_log` appends an object.  `overwrite_log` replaces the entire sequence
with one object.  `delete_log` removes the log.  These operations use CAT-SOOP
file locks, but they are still persistent changes; course reporting code
should normally be read-only.

Outside a CAT-SOOP request, import the module after arranging for the same
configuration environment as the server:

```python
# Run this program with CATSOOP_CONFIG set before Python starts.
from pprint import pprint
from catsoop import cslog

path = ["numerical_methods", "homework", "hw01"]
entries = cslog.read_log("ada", path, "problemactions")
pprint(entries)
```

For encrypted installations, `CATSOOP_PASSPHRASE` must likewise be present
before `catsoop.cslog` is imported.

<catsoop-section>Choosing an Access Method</catsoop-section>

Use `catsoop logread` for one-off inspection or conversion to plain text.  Use
`csm_cslog` in course reports and administrative pages that need structured
Python values.  Use `catsoop.tutor.compute_page_stats(...)` when a report needs
the standard combination of question metadata, current state, action history,
and manual grades; the next page explains those problem-specific records.
